1
00:00:00,166 --> 00:00:02,168
[narrator] Previously on The Big Bang Theory:

2
00:00:02,252 --> 00:00:05,296
-Guess I'm your new neighbor. Penny.
-Oh. Leonard, Sheldon.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:06,631
-Hi.
-[both] Hi.

4
00:00:06,715 --> 00:00:08,425
-Hi.
-Hi.

5
00:00:08,925 --> 00:00:11,136
-That's where I sit.
-Howard Wolowitz,

6
00:00:11,219 --> 00:00:16,474
-Caltech Department of Applied Physics.
-Penny. Leonard. Raj.

7
00:00:16,558 --> 00:00:19,227
[Howard] He speaks English.
He just can't speak to women.

8
00:00:19,310 --> 00:00:22,647
Our babies will be smart and beautiful.
Finally caught a break.

9
00:00:22,731 --> 00:00:24,691
♪ Soft kitty ♪

10
00:00:24,774 --> 00:00:28,403
-You know my girlfriend, Bernadette.
-[Mrs. Wolowitz] Howard, I'm home!

11
00:00:28,486 --> 00:00:30,447
♪ Warm kitty ♪

12
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:32,198
[screaming]

13
00:00:32,741 --> 00:00:34,993
♪ Little ball of fur ♪

14
00:00:35,076 --> 00:00:37,078
I'm Amy Farrah Fowler.
You're Sheldon Cooper.

15
00:00:39,372 --> 00:00:40,665
-Bazinga.
-Whoa!

16
00:00:40,749 --> 00:00:41,791
[horns honking]

17
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:44,502
-[Penny screams]
-[Sheldon screams]

18
00:00:45,628 --> 00:00:49,674
Welcome to the premier episode
of Sheldon Cooper Presents: Fun with Flags.

19
00:00:49,758 --> 00:00:53,303
I'm going up in space.
Technically, I'm an astronaut.

20
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:55,597
Oy vey!

21
00:00:56,598 --> 00:00:58,516
You're talking to me.

22
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,059
-I am.
-[gasps]

23
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:04,773
Bernadette Maryann Rostenkowski...

24
00:01:04,856 --> 00:01:09,110
[all] We now pronounce you
husband and wife.

25
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:12,447
Amy. Howard. Angry Amy.

26
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:15,867
I don't want you to say it just
because social convention dictates--

27
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:18,286
-I love you too.
-Will you marry me?

28
00:01:18,369 --> 00:01:19,621
Yes.

29
00:01:19,704 --> 00:01:20,747
We did it!

30
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:23,166
Well, I enjoyed that
more than I thought I would.

31
00:01:24,834 --> 00:01:26,127
I wonder which one's Halley.

32
00:01:26,211 --> 00:01:28,213
[baby squalling in nasal voice]

33
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:34,844
-Will you marry me?
-That means positive?

34
00:01:34,928 --> 00:01:38,181
Super-asymmetry, that's it.

35
00:01:38,264 --> 00:01:40,892
Our paper was right. We did it.

36
00:01:40,975 --> 00:01:43,019
-We did it?
-We did it.

37
00:01:44,437 --> 00:01:47,732
♪ Six times 10 to the eighth atoms
Of radium in a lead jar ♪

38
00:01:47,816 --> 00:01:50,485
♪ Six times 10 to the eighth atoms of radium ♪

39
00:01:50,568 --> 00:01:52,612
♪ If the half-life of radium
Should happen to pass ♪

40
00:01:52,695 --> 00:01:55,907
♪ Three times 10 to the eighth atoms
Of radium in a lead jar ♪

41
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:58,159
Really hope you're almost to zero.

42
00:01:58,243 --> 00:02:03,164
That's the beauty of half-lives. It's impossible
to determine when you'll arrive at zero.

43
00:02:03,248 --> 00:02:06,918
It's like "Wheels on the Bus"
if the bus had an unknowable number of parts.

44
00:02:08,461 --> 00:02:11,589
-Yeah, we're gonna need more coffee.
-Yep, I'm with you.

45
00:02:11,673 --> 00:02:14,717
-Leonard, coffee?
-Black and strong like Luke Cage.

46
00:02:16,344 --> 00:02:19,055
Too tired to even be disturbed by that.

47
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:21,224
-Sheldon?
-Absolutely not.

48
00:02:21,307 --> 00:02:25,144
The Nobel Committee will be making the calls
to inform the winners at any minute,

49
00:02:25,228 --> 00:02:28,148
so the only drug I need is the endorphins
pumping through my brain

50
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:29,941
in anticipation of our victory.

51
00:02:30,024 --> 00:02:34,237
Well, technically, anticipation wouldn't be
mediated by endorphins as much dopamine,

52
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,990
but you've been up all night,
so I'll give you that one.

53
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:43,788
Really? The second he stops talking?

54
00:02:43,872 --> 00:02:45,957
Well, should we wake him up?

55
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:49,961
He did say if he fell asleep,
we were allowed to slap him awake.

56
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:53,506
Oh, boy.

57
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:57,343
-Wait a minute, why do you get to do it?
-I called it.

58
00:02:57,427 --> 00:03:00,805
-You can't just call it. You have to earn it.
-Oh, and you've earned it?

59
00:03:00,889 --> 00:03:03,725
No one has earned it more than me.

60
00:03:05,476 --> 00:03:06,936
[Amy clears throat]

61
00:03:09,063 --> 00:03:12,567
You have your whole life to smack him around.
This is my time.

62
00:03:14,235 --> 00:03:15,445
Fine, don't miss.

63
00:03:15,528 --> 00:03:17,864
It's not a volleyball. I can handle it.

64
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:20,825
[Penny] Oh, wait, hang on.

65
00:03:20,909 --> 00:03:23,119
When you're old,
you are gonna want a record of this.

66
00:03:23,202 --> 00:03:26,915
Oh, yeah, do it in slow-mo.
I wanna see his cheeks ripple.

67
00:03:28,541 --> 00:03:29,918
Ready, go.

68
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:32,128
-[cell phone ringing]
-[gasps] It's happening!

69
00:03:32,211 --> 00:03:35,757
-Unknown caller. It's gotta be them.
-Put it on speaker.

70
00:03:36,799 --> 00:03:38,009
-Hello.
-Hi.

71
00:03:38,092 --> 00:03:40,220
[man with rhotacism]
Hello, this is Sweden calling.

72
00:03:42,388 --> 00:03:45,183
Is this Dr. Cooper and Dr. Fowler?

73
00:03:45,266 --> 00:03:46,517
-Yes.
-Yeah.

74
00:03:46,601 --> 00:03:49,479
Congratulations,
it is my pleasure to inform you

75
00:03:49,562 --> 00:03:53,441
that you've won
the Nobel Prize in being suckers!

76
00:03:57,737 --> 00:03:59,697
His Swedish accent was very convincing.

77
00:04:00,865 --> 00:04:03,034
-[cell phone ringing]
-[gasps] This is it.

78
00:04:04,619 --> 00:04:07,872
-What do you want, Howard?
-We were just calling to see if you'd heard yet.

79
00:04:07,956 --> 00:04:08,998
We haven't.

80
00:04:09,082 --> 00:04:12,335
But thank you for getting up so early to call.
That was very thoughtful.

81
00:04:12,418 --> 00:04:15,255
Oh, please, we have two little kids.
We've been up for an hour.

82
00:04:16,547 --> 00:04:19,842
-[Howard] Did anyone get to slap Sheldon?
-No.

83
00:04:19,926 --> 00:04:21,469
All right, call us when you hear.

84
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:25,056
All right, now what?

85
00:04:25,139 --> 00:04:29,477
Why don't we play a game to pass the time?
Here. I'm thinking of a number.

86
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:32,021
Hint: It's a cube of a cube of a prime.

87
00:04:32,105 --> 00:04:35,984
-There's an infinite number of possibilities.
-You got somewhere to be?

88
00:04:37,068 --> 00:04:39,112
-[cell phone ringing]
-Oh, that's me.

89
00:04:40,738 --> 00:04:41,656
Hello?

90
00:04:42,699 --> 00:04:44,284
Yes, this is Dr. Fowler.

91
00:04:46,828 --> 00:04:47,662
I see.

92
00:04:48,955 --> 00:04:49,998
Okay, thank you.

93
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:55,461
We won.

94
00:05:02,802 --> 00:05:04,262
Congratulations!

95
00:05:04,345 --> 00:05:05,763
Oh, my God.

96
00:05:05,847 --> 00:05:08,057
-We did it.
-[Amy] I know.

97
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:12,061
-Can you believe it?
-That's a good point. What if I'm dreaming?

98
00:05:12,145 --> 00:05:13,271
[Penny screams]

99
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:20,903
We won the Nobel Prize!

100
00:05:20,987 --> 00:05:22,989
[all screaming]

101
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:28,703
♪ Our whole universe was in
A hot, dense state ♪

102
00:05:28,786 --> 00:05:32,665
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years ago
Expansion started-- Wait ♪

103
00:05:32,749 --> 00:05:35,418
♪ The Earth began to cool
The autotrophs began to drool ♪

104
00:05:35,501 --> 00:05:37,712
♪ Neanderthals developed tools
We built a wall ♪

105
00:05:37,795 --> 00:05:38,921
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

106
00:05:39,005 --> 00:05:41,632
♪ Math, science, history
Unraveling the mystery ♪

107
00:05:41,716 --> 00:05:43,843
♪ It all started with a big bang ♪

108
00:05:43,926 --> 00:05:45,136
♪ Bang! ♪

109
00:05:49,807 --> 00:05:52,685
How does it feel to be married
to a Nobel Prize winner?

110
00:05:52,769 --> 00:05:53,728
You tell me.

111
00:05:53,811 --> 00:05:56,856
Oh. Amy-centric, what a fun way to look at it.

112
00:05:57,690 --> 00:05:59,942
-I think so.
-You would. That makes sense.

113
00:06:00,026 --> 00:06:02,153
-[cell phone buzzes]
-Ooh.

114
00:06:02,236 --> 00:06:05,698
Aw. It's a congratulations text
from my Meemaw.

115
00:06:05,782 --> 00:06:08,117
-[cell phone buzzes]
-Oh, there's one from my mom.

116
00:06:08,201 --> 00:06:09,660
-[cell phone buzzes]
-And my sister.

117
00:06:09,744 --> 00:06:11,162
-[cell phone buzzes]
-And my brother.

118
00:06:11,245 --> 00:06:13,039
-[cell phone buzzes]
-And my brother's ex-wife.

119
00:06:13,122 --> 00:06:15,500
-[cell phone buzzes]
-And my brother's other ex-wife.

120
00:06:15,583 --> 00:06:19,128
Boy, they don't tell you when you win
a Nobel it chews up your phone battery.

121
00:06:20,129 --> 00:06:21,923
-[cell phone buzzes]
-[Amy] Oh, that's me.

122
00:06:23,841 --> 00:06:26,219
It's CVS. My prescription's ready.

123
00:06:26,302 --> 00:06:27,220
[cell phone buzzes]

124
00:06:27,303 --> 00:06:30,890
Oh, and also my dad.
He says congratulations and he loves me.

125
00:06:30,973 --> 00:06:32,100
Nothing about me?

126
00:06:32,183 --> 00:06:33,226
-[cell phone buzzes]
-Oh.

127
00:06:33,309 --> 00:06:35,520
Oh, it's your dad. I'm good.

128
00:06:36,646 --> 00:06:39,565
You know, when you think about it,
now that we're Nobel Prize winners,

129
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:41,901
our names will be linked together forever.

130
00:06:41,984 --> 00:06:44,862
We're married.
Our names are already linked together forever.

131
00:06:44,946 --> 00:06:48,074
Please, that's just a piece of paper.
This is a piece of paper and a medal.

132
00:06:50,159 --> 00:06:54,664
It's weird, I don't really feel different,
but I guess our lives will never be the same.

133
00:06:54,747 --> 00:06:57,458
I don't know. We're going to work like always.

134
00:06:57,542 --> 00:07:00,169
I still put my pants on both legs at a time.

135
00:07:01,254 --> 00:07:03,673
One day that's gonna end very badly.

136
00:07:04,715 --> 00:07:07,760
-[reporter 1] Oh! Dr. Cooper!
-[reporter 2] Dr. Fowler!

137
00:07:07,844 --> 00:07:09,637
[reporters clamoring]

138
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,472
Congratulations. How does it feel?

139
00:07:11,556 --> 00:07:15,685
Okay, we're happy to answer your questions.
Just, um, one at a time, please.

140
00:07:19,438 --> 00:07:22,900
-Is Dr. Cooper coming back?
-No. Next question.

141
00:07:22,984 --> 00:07:24,569
[reporters] Dr. Fowler!

142
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:28,906
Was it your left hand or your right hand?

143
00:07:30,491 --> 00:07:31,450
Right.

144
00:07:31,534 --> 00:07:33,661
Spit actually flew out of his mouth.

145
00:07:35,371 --> 00:07:36,247
[Siebert] Hey, fellas.

146
00:07:38,249 --> 00:07:40,418
-Can you do me a favor?
-Do we have a choice?

147
00:07:41,586 --> 00:07:42,545
No.

148
00:07:43,754 --> 00:07:45,882
Sheldon and Amy are now officially superstars,

149
00:07:45,965 --> 00:07:49,343
and the press will be reaching out
to their family and friends.

150
00:07:49,427 --> 00:07:54,515
So that we're all on the same page, the word
we're gonna use to describe them is "quirky."

151
00:07:54,599 --> 00:07:58,936
-And not "quirky." More like "quirky."
-So not Mr. and Mrs. Wackadoodle?

152
00:08:00,897 --> 00:08:04,066
You bitter, envious little man.

153
00:08:08,738 --> 00:08:10,573
Thank you so much.

154
00:08:15,161 --> 00:08:17,038
And what do we call that?

155
00:08:17,121 --> 00:08:18,581
[all] Quirky.

156
00:08:21,626 --> 00:08:24,253
Dr. Cooper, can I get a minute of your time?

157
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,006
Hello?

158
00:08:27,089 --> 00:08:29,217
Hey, can I help you?

159
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:33,638
Yeah, actually, um, I had an appointment
to interview Dr. Cooper about the Nobel.

160
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:35,389
Hang on a second.

161
00:08:35,473 --> 00:08:36,307
Sheldon?

162
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:44,273
Sorry, he's not here.

163
00:08:45,691 --> 00:08:47,902
Damn, I've got a deadline.

164
00:08:47,985 --> 00:08:52,657
I don't know if it helps you at all,
but I'm his best friend in the whole world.

165
00:08:54,367 --> 00:08:55,576
-Really?
-And an astronaut.

166
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:58,538
Come on, you can buy me a cup of coffee
and I'll tell you about both.

167
00:09:00,623 --> 00:09:02,625
[Amy sobbing]

168
00:09:08,005 --> 00:09:09,715
Everyone okay in there?

169
00:09:09,799 --> 00:09:10,841
[Amy] Go away.

170
00:09:12,009 --> 00:09:13,386
Amy?

171
00:09:13,469 --> 00:09:15,263
Raj, please, not now.

172
00:09:15,346 --> 00:09:16,806
Hey, what's wrong?

173
00:09:17,974 --> 00:09:21,102
My picture's all over the Internet
and I look terrible.

174
00:09:21,185 --> 00:09:23,437
No, let me see.

175
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:25,898
Well, that is an unfortunate angle.

176
00:09:27,066 --> 00:09:27,984
But who cares?

177
00:09:28,067 --> 00:09:31,404
You just won the Nobel.
You should be proud of this moment.

178
00:09:31,487 --> 00:09:34,657
I know I shouldn't care about how I look
and I never thought I did.

179
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:38,077
It's stupid and shallow, but I just can't help it.

180
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:40,579
Am I really this frumpy?

181
00:09:40,663 --> 00:09:44,875
No. No, you are a beautiful woman.

182
00:09:44,959 --> 00:09:48,087
If you're not happy with those pictures,
then make some changes.

183
00:09:48,170 --> 00:09:52,341
Get a haircut, new clothes,
new glasses, big glasses--

184
00:09:52,425 --> 00:09:55,011
No glasses.
Then you won't be able to see those pictures.

185
00:09:56,637 --> 00:09:58,639
Sounds expensive.

186
00:09:58,723 --> 00:10:03,269
Excuse me, if I'm not mistaken, the Nobel
comes with a substantial cash prize.

187
00:10:03,352 --> 00:10:05,271
What were you gonna spend it on?

188
00:10:05,938 --> 00:10:08,107
I hadn't really thought about it.

189
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:10,693
Sheldon's got his eyes on some new Dockers.

190
00:10:12,278 --> 00:10:14,488
Come on, do something for yourself.

191
00:10:14,572 --> 00:10:17,658
Well, I suppose I could get a haircut.

192
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:20,995
And some makeup, and a new wardrobe,

193
00:10:21,078 --> 00:10:23,289
and a little thank-you gift
for your shopping buddy

194
00:10:23,372 --> 00:10:25,708
if we see something he likes. Come on.

195
00:10:25,791 --> 00:10:29,712
-Where are we going?
-Beverly Hills, where the things he likes are.

196
00:10:33,382 --> 00:10:34,342
Dinner ready?

197
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:35,885
Not unless you cooked.

198
00:10:38,471 --> 00:10:41,515
Why is this article
about Sheldon all about you?

199
00:10:43,267 --> 00:10:44,602
Let me see.

200
00:10:44,685 --> 00:10:46,812
Oh, good, they used my NASA picture.

201
00:10:48,689 --> 00:10:52,026
Why does it say that you're his best friend?
Leonard's his best friend.

202
00:10:52,109 --> 00:10:55,613
No, Leonard has always been
kind of a monkey butler.

203
00:10:56,947 --> 00:11:01,035
Whenever Sheldon got into a scrape,
I was his go-to guy.

204
00:11:01,118 --> 00:11:03,079
When did he get into a scrape?

205
00:11:03,162 --> 00:11:04,497
You're kidding, right?

206
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:07,708
Remember when he had a panic attack
because his hand got stuck in a jar of olives?

207
00:11:07,792 --> 00:11:10,628
I was the one who told him
to let go of the olives.

208
00:11:13,381 --> 00:11:15,549
Please, that doesn't make you his best friend.

209
00:11:15,633 --> 00:11:17,259
[scoffs]

210
00:11:17,343 --> 00:11:22,848
You know, that reporter asked me if I could
put him in touch with Amy's best friend.

211
00:11:22,932 --> 00:11:25,601
-That's Penny.
-[singsong] Doesn't have to be.

212
00:11:31,273 --> 00:11:32,817
Hmm.

213
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:36,487
What kind of tea is appropriate
for winning a Nobel Prize

214
00:11:36,570 --> 00:11:40,408
and now everything is changing
and you feel unmoored from reality?

215
00:11:41,867 --> 00:11:43,119
I don't know, Earl Grey?

216
00:11:45,329 --> 00:11:47,748
You know, this is something
I've wanted my whole life,

217
00:11:47,832 --> 00:11:50,334
but I never considered
how everything would be different.

218
00:11:50,418 --> 00:11:54,922
I know it all feels overwhelming right now,
but I promise you things will settle down.

219
00:11:55,005 --> 00:11:57,591
There's no Earl Grey, you filthy liar.

220
00:11:59,009 --> 00:12:00,553
[knocking on door]

221
00:12:00,636 --> 00:12:02,555
-Hey, is Penny here?
-No, why?

222
00:12:02,638 --> 00:12:05,224
I wanted to show her my latest creation.

223
00:12:05,307 --> 00:12:06,392
I give you...

224
00:12:07,393 --> 00:12:09,353
Dr. Amy Farrah Fowler.

225
00:12:16,402 --> 00:12:18,237
Wow, Amy, you look amazing.

226
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,072
Thank you.

227
00:12:20,156 --> 00:12:22,491
Sheldon, what do you think?

228
00:12:27,913 --> 00:12:30,332
I like you better the way you were.

229
00:12:30,416 --> 00:12:33,085
But she looks beautiful.

230
00:12:33,169 --> 00:12:36,505
Classic lines,
colors that complement her skin tone,

231
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:40,134
and hair that goes from "office"
to "on the town" in minutes.

232
00:12:43,179 --> 00:12:45,181
I don't care. Put it back.

233
00:12:45,264 --> 00:12:48,017
-I like the way I look.
-Well, I don't.

234
00:12:50,769 --> 00:12:53,230
My fault. I was out of Earl Grey. [clears throat]

235
00:12:55,357 --> 00:12:57,193
Sheldon, that was really rude.

236
00:12:57,276 --> 00:13:01,447
I'm sorry. Amy's the one constant
I can count on and now she's changing.

237
00:13:01,530 --> 00:13:03,741
It's just a haircut and some clothes.

238
00:13:03,824 --> 00:13:06,452
No, it's the last straw. I can't take any more.

239
00:13:06,535 --> 00:13:07,786
[elevator bell dings]

240
00:13:15,085 --> 00:13:17,254
Can you believe it?
They finally fixed the elevator.

241
00:13:20,758 --> 00:13:22,176
This is a nightmare.

242
00:13:23,219 --> 00:13:24,053
What's with him?

243
00:13:24,136 --> 00:13:26,347
He won a Nobel Prize
and his wife looks amazing.

244
00:13:26,430 --> 00:13:28,224
Oh, yeah, got it.

245
00:13:44,698 --> 00:13:46,700
[gasping]

246
00:13:49,453 --> 00:13:52,873
-How did you get down here?
-Elevator, it's really fast.

247
00:13:54,083 --> 00:13:56,502
I need to be alone right now.
Don't try to follow me.

248
00:13:56,585 --> 00:13:59,088
-All right. Do you need a ride?
-That'd be great, thank you.

249
00:14:01,924 --> 00:14:03,425
I should've seen this coming.

250
00:14:03,509 --> 00:14:08,806
-Oh, stop, you're allowed to get a haircut.
-I know, but I should've done it gradually.

251
00:14:08,889 --> 00:14:09,890
You know, like...

252
00:14:13,018 --> 00:14:16,355
maybe 300 tiny haircuts over a 10-year period.

253
00:14:17,898 --> 00:14:20,526
Okay, you need to focus on the positive.

254
00:14:20,609 --> 00:14:23,863
You won a Nobel Prize. I slapped Sheldon.

255
00:14:23,946 --> 00:14:25,990
A lot of dreams came true today.

256
00:14:27,741 --> 00:14:30,077
You know, you're right.

257
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:31,954
This is a huge day for me,

258
00:14:32,037 --> 00:14:35,833
and I'm allowed to enjoy it without worrying
how it's going to affect my husband.

259
00:14:37,042 --> 00:14:39,878
Is it me or did it just get fierce in here?

260
00:14:44,425 --> 00:14:47,970
All this change is just too much.

261
00:14:48,053 --> 00:14:52,099
The reporters, the attention at work,
and now even Amy's changed.

262
00:14:53,726 --> 00:14:54,935
Whoa, whoa, whoa. Slow down.

263
00:14:55,019 --> 00:14:59,315
I'm playing a drinking game.
Every time you say "change," I take a slug.

264
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:05,696
-Are you gonna be able to drive me home?
-Mm, not unless you change the subject. Oh.

265
00:15:05,779 --> 00:15:07,281
Now I said it. Wah.

266
00:15:11,952 --> 00:15:14,705
You're the only person
who could win the biggest prize in science

267
00:15:14,788 --> 00:15:17,625
-and still be upset about it.
-It's just...

268
00:15:17,708 --> 00:15:19,627
all the times I thought about winning,

269
00:15:19,710 --> 00:15:23,380
I never thought about how it would ch--
Affect my life.

270
00:15:25,257 --> 00:15:27,426
I'm sorry,
I'm genuinely concerned about your liver.

271
00:15:27,509 --> 00:15:28,886
Okay.

272
00:15:28,969 --> 00:15:31,430
Fine, I'll stop playing.

273
00:15:31,513 --> 00:15:33,891
You go on and on about
wanting things to stay the same,

274
00:15:33,974 --> 00:15:36,226
but you've changed a lot since I met you.

275
00:15:36,310 --> 00:15:38,270
Oh, you are a mean drunk.

276
00:15:39,313 --> 00:15:40,689
I'm serious.

277
00:15:40,773 --> 00:15:43,317
You have a ton of friends, you got married,

278
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:47,321
moved into a new apartment,
you wore a baseball hat that one time.

279
00:15:47,404 --> 00:15:50,908
Heck, you've had sex almost
as many times as I have fingers.

280
00:15:53,702 --> 00:15:55,204
-More.
-[Penny] Wha...

281
00:15:55,287 --> 00:15:56,455
By this many.

282
00:15:56,538 --> 00:15:58,123
You dog.

283
00:15:58,207 --> 00:16:01,627
-It was the Avengers trailer.
-Oh. Mm-hm.

284
00:16:01,710 --> 00:16:03,921
I've grown too.
I used to be the bartender back there.

285
00:16:04,004 --> 00:16:04,880
[Sheldon] That's true.

286
00:16:04,964 --> 00:16:08,634
And now there's a completely different woman
who botched my drink order.

287
00:16:08,717 --> 00:16:11,762
How hard is 65 percent Coke,
35 percent Diet Coke?

288
00:16:13,138 --> 00:16:16,392
Judging by the look on her face,
it's at least 1 percent saliva.

289
00:16:20,604 --> 00:16:25,234
But I do take your point. You're also married,
you have a successful career,

290
00:16:25,317 --> 00:16:28,278
you no longer dress like you're
trying to attract sailors by the wharf.

291
00:16:30,531 --> 00:16:33,575
So I guess the only thing
that actually stays the same

292
00:16:33,659 --> 00:16:35,995
is that things are always changing.

293
00:16:37,329 --> 00:16:38,956
Interesting.

294
00:16:39,039 --> 00:16:44,586
So you're saying the inevitability of change
might be a universal constant.

295
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:48,424
Well, there's a little more
to it than that but, yeah, sure.

296
00:16:49,466 --> 00:16:51,260
Oh, hey, look, it's Bernadette.

297
00:16:51,343 --> 00:16:55,806
I can't tell you how many times
Dr. Fowler was gonna give up,

298
00:16:55,889 --> 00:16:59,226
and I would say to her,
"Amy, as your best friend,

299
00:16:59,309 --> 00:17:00,936
I am not gonna let you quit."

300
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:02,521
Okay, I'm drinking again.

301
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:06,316
I'll join you.
Waitress, 95 percent Hawaiian Punch,

302
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:07,776
5 percent vodka.

303
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:11,447
Let me tell you about the time
Mr. Nobel Laureate wanted olives.

304
00:17:11,530 --> 00:17:13,615
This is a good one.

305
00:17:13,699 --> 00:17:14,950
You know what, 90/10.

306
00:17:19,371 --> 00:17:22,958
You know, I like the way my hair looks.
I'm done tiptoeing around him.

307
00:17:23,041 --> 00:17:24,710
We're all guilty of it.

308
00:17:24,793 --> 00:17:26,086
But why?

309
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:28,672
-We were afraid to upset him.
-Which happens anyway.

310
00:17:28,756 --> 00:17:31,842
-Well, that's over.
-I'm done enabling him.

311
00:17:31,925 --> 00:17:37,765
Like this is his spot, and the thermostat
has to be set to his comfort level

312
00:17:37,848 --> 00:17:41,351
even though he doesn't even live here
and I'm always chilly.

313
00:17:44,313 --> 00:17:46,315
Is that why you wear a hoodie all the time?

314
00:17:48,108 --> 00:17:49,651
Why would--? Yes!

315
00:17:51,236 --> 00:17:54,823
To accommodate Sheldon.
And what about this thing?

316
00:17:54,907 --> 00:17:56,658
Why is it here? I'll tell you why.

317
00:17:56,742 --> 00:17:59,995
Because it was here when I moved in,
and for no earthly reason,

318
00:18:00,079 --> 00:18:02,122
he forbade me to touch it.

319
00:18:02,206 --> 00:18:04,833
Well, if you don't like it, get rid of it.

320
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:06,376
Put it in the closet.

321
00:18:07,336 --> 00:18:09,630
You know what, I will.

322
00:18:18,222 --> 00:18:19,640
I bet that's the reason.

323
00:18:27,147 --> 00:18:27,981
Hang on.

324
00:18:32,736 --> 00:18:34,696
What do you think? Wanna give it a try?

325
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:36,406
[elevator bell dings]

326
00:18:49,002 --> 00:18:53,048
Well, the elevator did work
when I moved into the building,

327
00:18:53,132 --> 00:18:56,093
so going up and down the stairs was a change,

328
00:18:56,176 --> 00:18:59,138
which means this would actually
be a return to the status quo.

329
00:18:59,221 --> 00:19:01,265
-But conversely--
-Get in.

330
00:19:08,355 --> 00:19:10,023
[Sheldon] This is wild.

